# .gitattributes
# Line endings, decided here rather than per-machine.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Without it, what lands in a working tree depends on each
# clone's `core.autocrlf`, which is a per-developer setting nobody checks. This
# repository is developed on Windows and deployed to Linux, which is exactly the
# combination where that goes wrong -- and it goes wrong in a way that costs an
# hour, because the error names neither the file nor the cause:
#
#     -bash: ./start.sh: /usr/bin/env: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# That is a CRLF `start.sh`. The kernel reads the shebang line including the
# trailing \r, looks for an interpreter literally named "bash\r", and reports it
# missing -- without ever mentioning carriage returns. systemd fails the same
# way, with `Failed to execute command: No such file or directory` for an
# ExecStart that is plainly right there.
#
# Nothing is wrong today: `git show :start.sh | file -` confirms the index holds
# LF. But that is a consequence of this machine's core.autocrlf=true, not a
# property of the repository, and it would not survive one clone configured
# differently or one file committed through a tool that does not normalise.

# Default: normalise text to LF inside the repository, and check it out in the
# platform's native form. This is the right default for source that no OS cares
# about the endings of -- .py, .ts, .tsx, .css, .md, .json.
* text=auto

# --- must be LF in the WORKING TREE, on every platform -----------------------
# These are read by a Linux kernel or by systemd, both of which treat \r as part
# of the token rather than as whitespace. `eol=lf` overrides core.autocrlf, so
# they stay correct even on a Windows checkout.
*.sh         text eol=lf
*.service    text eol=lf
*.socket     text eol=lf
*.timer      text eol=lf
Dockerfile   text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf

# nginx configuration. nginx itself tolerates CRLF, but the files are edited on
# the server as often as in the repository and mixed endings in one file are
# worse than either convention.
*.conf       text eol=lf

# --- must be CRLF, for the opposite reason -----------------------------------
# PowerShell handles LF fine when running a script, but Windows tooling that
# opens these for editing does not all agree, and start.ps1 is the entry point a
# Windows developer actually touches. Keep it native.
*.ps1        text eol=crlf
*.bat        text eol=crlf
*.cmd        text eol=crlf

# --- never touch -------------------------------------------------------------
# Marked explicitly so no heuristic ever decides one of these is text and
# "normalises" bytes in the middle of it. The spreadsheet is a tracked INPUT to
# ingestion, not a build artifact -- corrupting it would silently change the
# player pool.
*.xlsx  binary
*.xls   binary
*.db    binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.webp  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.glb   binary
*.gltf  binary
